    {
      "kind": "interpretive_questions",
      "title": "Women, Teaching, and Childbearing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "block_4",
          "text": "Before the restriction in verse 12, the text explicitly tells a woman to learn in quietness and full submission. The rare verb often translated \u201cexercise authority\u201d occurs here alongside teaching, and its precise force is disputed: proposed meanings range from ordinary authority to domineering or unauthorized action. Readers also disagree about whether the command addresses a local situation or establishes a universal rule.",
          "verse_refs": [
            "1 Timothy 2:11-12",
            "1 Timothy 2:12",
            "1 Timothy 2:5-7",
            "1 Timothy 2:7",
            "1 Timothy 2:9-10"
          ],
          "evidence_ids": [
            "1-timothy:interpretive_note:0",
            "1-timothy:interpretive_note:10",
            "1-timothy:interpretive_note:11",
            "1-timothy:interpretive_note:12",
            "1-timothy:interpretive_note:13"
          ],
          "synthesis_ids": [
            "syn_interpretive_questions_23567e78dc09"
          ],
          "confidence": "low",
          "interpretation_level": "disputed"
        },
        {
          "id": "block_5",
          "text": "The appeal to Adam\u2019s formation and Eve\u2019s deception is part of the argument, but interpreters dispute how it supports the preceding instruction. The final statement about being saved through childbearing is likewise unresolved and should not be read as making pregnancy necessary, safe, or a remedy for sin.",
          "verse_refs": [
            "1 Timothy 2:12",
            "1 Timothy 2:13-14",
            "1 Timothy 2:15",
            "1 Timothy 2:3-7",
            "1 Timothy 2:9-10"
          ],
